  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/66/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_54.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A man named El Toro, a Communist-supported candidate, has just been elected president of Santo Rico.&nbsp; The election of a \"Red\" doesn't make sense to Hank.&nbsp; Why would the people of Santo Rico <i>do</i> such a thing?&nbsp; Jan doesn't care and only wants to discuss vacation plans, even threatening to \"smash a few of your silly ol' test tubes\" if Hank doesn't agree to it.\n\nWashington summons Giant-Man and the Wasp because they feel the election was won by \"foul means.\"&nbsp; They want the pair to pretend to tour Santo Rico, all the while searching for evidence that the election was stolen by Communists.\n\nBut upon arrival, they're immediately deemed \"suspicious\" by the \"police state\" that is watching everything now, and the newly-elected El Toro directs his men to abduct Jan.&nbsp; As she is the one holding all of the pair's size-changing capsules in her handbag, she has time only to toss a single growth capsule in Hank's direction, which allows him to become Giant-Man.\n\nBut his larger size proves to be a disadvantage due to the fact that he becomes all that much easier to spot, and the local police soon give chase.&nbsp; Giant-Man blunders around the city, bumping into food stands, moving cars, and telephone wires, all while the local populace continues to report his whereabouts, all in fear of retribution by the new Communist governor.\n\nHank slips by the authorities on top of a train that carries him to where their luggage was sent.&nbsp; There, he finds the cybernetic helmet that allows him to communicate with the ants, and it is <i>they</i> who eventually locate Jan for him.\n\nHank goes to rescue Jan, gets access again to the pills in her purse, and while Giant-Man evades the charging El Toro, the Wasp finds proof in the records that the election was financed by the Reds!&nbsp; The citizens are outraged and call him a traitor!&nbsp; The day is saved.&nbsp; Thanks to them, \"Santo Rico is again a democratic nation!\"\n\nDespite the travel, Jan still insists they need a vacation.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A Communist steals an election but then takes most of the issue off.\nJan is captured, and Giant-Man blunders about looking for the missing plot.",
  "subtitle": "The Vanishing Villain of Santo Rico",
  "review_text": "Another [SPAN: severity=error; text=disappointing outing] from a magazine whose latest installment felt every bit like the writer and artist were merely going through the motions just to fill up 13 pages.\n\n[ASSET: left | SmashTestTubes | width=15%] Right from the start, Jan kept trying to get Hank to think about going on a vacation.&nbsp; Need I remind you that they just recently <i>returned</i> from a vacation to Greece back in [LINK:1963:TTA_46_ant0|TTA #46|2026-10-02], published back in May 1963?\n[BR]\n[BR] Of course, the way this book [SPAN: severity=warning; text=burns through months of story time in one sitting] (e.g. [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47|2026-10-23] and [LINK:1963:TTA_52_giant0|TTA #52|2027-03-07]) means that more time has likely elapsed for the pair than the publication schedule would otherwise suggest.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Who can actually <i>say</i> how long it has been since their last vacation?]\n\n[ASSET: right | Gorgeous | width=10%] It <i>is</i> astonishing how quickly the relationship between these two has already changed.&nbsp; Hank seems to have abandoned all pretense of propriety and virtue, referring to Jan as all of the following on page 3 <i>alone</i> (and even proposes they \"kiss and make up\"):\n[BR] [HANG] * \"doll\"\n[BR] [HANG] * \"Jan baby\"\n[BR] [HANG] * \"gorgeous\"\n[BR] [HANG] * [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Jan even calls Hank \"lover boy\" -- though that is actually on the <i>next</i> page)]\n\nWhile it seems ever more inevitable that these two end up together, in <i>reality</i>, their opening spat did nothing more than chew up the first three pages of the story (very much in the same vein as the Ben versus Johnny bickering we get on almost a monthly basis over in <i>Fantastic Four</i>).&nbsp; In a full-sized magazine, there is admittedly <i>room</i> for this, but here, with only 13 pages available, it only helps highlight how shallow the rest of the plot was.\n\nThe fan club that barges in right afterward wastes yet <i>another</i> page before the pair even <i>get</i> to Washington DC.&nbsp; And it isn't until the bottom of page 5 that Hank and Jan finally arrive in Santo Rico, pretending to be \"tourists.\"\n\nAt this point, there's only [SPAN: severity=warning; text=eight pages left!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Stan, you're telling me you're going to cram a story worth reading into eight pages?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I'm not <i>buying</i> it!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nSadly, though readers could not possibly <i>know</i> this, the \"good\" part of the story was [SPAN: severity=error; text=already over!]&nbsp; What followed was little more than filler...\n\n[ASSET: left | AmericanSpies | width=13%] [ASSET: right | HeCantHide | width=27%] The unimaginative El Toro finally appears on page 6 and <i>immediately</i> disappoints with his declaration that these two visiting Americans <i>must</i> be spies!\n[BR]\n[BR] His name \"El Toro\" means \"The Bull\" in English, and it turns out that his headgear is not only for show, as he plows into Giant-Man's back upon their first encounter before finally directing his men to find... and <i>kill</i>... Giant-Man.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=Without further ado, El Toro <b>doesn't appear again</b> until the second-to-last page of the story.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe majority of pages 7-12 are spent showing Giant-Man bumbling about, trying to elude the local authorities in absolutely [SPAN: severity=warning; text=yawn-worthy fashion.]\n\n[HANG0] * <i>Of course</i> he eventually locates Jan with the help of his ant spy net.\n[HANG0] * <i>Of course</i>, they're conveniently able to find records of the stolen election.\n[HANG0] * <i>Of course</i>, the charging El Toro proves to be no match for Giant-Man's skill.\n\n[ASSET: left | WeveBeenCheated | width=11%] The people of Santo Rico are justifiably upset that their election was stolen by the Communists and demand a new and fair election!&nbsp; Ultimately, the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved!]\n[BR]\n[BR] (Reader sanity is likewise restored after the highway <i>robbery</i> of their 12 cents.\n[BR] Santo Rico may need a new <i>election</i>, but this magazine needs a new <i>plot</i>!)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n\"Lackluster\" doesn't even <i>begin</i> to describe this story (or this magazine).&nbsp; This book proves to be a major disappointment almost every time I read it, and it's no wonder that I've nicknamed it [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Tales to Abolish] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(buyer beware)].\n\nThe story was uninteresting, the \"villain\" <i>barely</i> more than a plot device, and the action felt like an effort to punch in for the month.\n\nI have to ask:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=What did this story actually amount to?]\n[HANG] * A Communist steals an election in the far-off country of Santo Rico\n[HANG] * Hank and Jan bicker about going on vacation\n[HANG] * They are sent to investigate by the US government\n[HANG] * Jan is immediately captured by El Toro's men, leaving Hank stuck at giant-size\n[HANG] * Hank blunders about and finds that being large comes with certain disadvantages (though we already <i>saw</i> some version of this only four months earlier, back in [LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-14] -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=there is nothing new to be found here])\n[HANG] * Hank eventually finds Jan (who was scheduled to be executed, as mentioned on page 12)\n[HANG] * The two shrink down and escape notice of El Toro's men\n[HANG] * Hank becomes Giant-Man again and dodges the charging El Toro in unimpressive fashion\n[HANG] * They find the evidence of the stolen election -- the people will hold a new election!\n[HANG] * Their efforts are praised by the US government, happy that Santo Rico is a democratic nation again.\n[HANG] * Jan reminds Hank that they still need to plan that vacation...\n\nAlong the way, the cover-villain El Toro appears in a [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=grand total of 8 panels] (starting on page 6 of 13).\n\nThe cover asks:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=\"WHO IS <b>EL TORO?</b>\"]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But the story itself doesn't even seem to care... so why should <i>we</i>?)]\n\nEven what I called \"good\" -- the early bits about the vacation and their complicated relationship -- even <b><i>that</i></b> felt like filler in a story that didn't seem to know what it was doing, and in the end, I wonder how many readers actually stayed awake long enough to make it to the story's reprise of this in the closing moments...\n\nMy advice is simple:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Skip this one and move on...] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(and be glad you did!)]",
  "side_note": [
    "Note that the full-page \"Two More Triumphs for Marvel\" notice near the back announces [LINK:1964:FF_25|FF #25|2027-05-10] and Daredevil #1, but the text reads \"DARE-DEVIL\" (with the hyphen).&nbsp; It's not stated that they're \"now on sale\" (since I suppose they hadn't yet come out).",
    "<b>The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale:</b>  \"Conquest\"\n[BR] (by Stan Lee, Larry Lieber, and Sol Brodsky)\n\nJan has to turn down an offer from Hank for a night out because she's promised to watch the son of a sick friend.&nbsp; While she is there, young Tommy asks her to tell him a bedtime story.\n\nThe story involves Shann, the ruler of a \"planet far away.\"&nbsp; The people there are so happy, he fears they will take him for granted and so decides to become \"Shann, the Conqueror\" and orders them to invade a planet on his behalf.\n\nThe invasion goes well but leads to Shann and his people having to support the weaker Andromian people, which causes his own people to resent ever having invaded.&nbsp; Their quality of life has been disrupted because of the extra burden.&nbsp; Shann should have been content to leave things the way they were.\n\n[HANG0] Tommy has only one question for \"Aunt Jan\":\n[BR] \"How come you don't have your <i>wasp's wings</i> when you're normal-sized?\"\n\nJan can only sigh in disbelief...."
  ],
  "highlight": "Giant-Man races across Santo Rico to rescue the Wasp and expose El Toro's election fraud.&nbsp; Readers may be less successful in finding the plot."
}